    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pancreatic cancer remains a highly lethal malignancy as the third leading cause of cancer-related deaths in the United States. In the majority of cases, patients are diagnosed at an advanced stage as current pancreatic cancer biomarkers suffer from poor sensitivity and specificity. Few studies look at detectable molecular alterations at the pre-cancerous level, thus, warranting research into early detection strategies. Glycosylation is an important post-translational modification that is aberrant in cancer. With a role in cancer growth and metastasis, altered glycosylation can be exploited for novel pancreatic cancer biomarker identification. Mass spectrometry-based glycoproteomics allows for the study of glycosylated proteins from cysts that are often precursor lesions to pancreatic cancer. Using an in-depth glycoproteomic workflow that enriches for glycopeptides, this study aimed to assess glycosylation profiles between cysts that have high malignant potential from cysts that have low malignant potential. Across fifteen cyst fluid samples from patients with various types of cysts including mucinous cystic neoplasms (MCN), intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMN), adenocarcinomas, and pseudocysts, 425 unique glycoproteins were identified. Of these glycoproteins, many were commonly found in the pancreas, including MUC5AC and CEACAM5. Unprecedented glycosylation was seen on the GP2 protein, a protein exclusive to pancreatic acinar cells, with 78 different sugar structures found on a single glycosylation site. This dataset provides novel information about glycan composition at specific protein sites in pancreatic cyst fluid with the majority of the glycosites having not been previously mapped. The results of this study reveal the diversity of protein glycosylation present on pancreas-derived proteins, allowing for better comparison of glycosylation profiles across different cyst fluid sample types that may correlate with particular diagnoses or disease states.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
